WHICH                          OS-9/68000                          WHICHNAME     which -- what a command name runs, and where it came fromSYNOPSIS     which [-i] [-a] [--] [command]DESCRIPTION     which says what the shell would run for command.  It looks where     Microware's shell looks, in the same order:       1. the module directory -- a module already in memory       2. the execution directory (chx)       3. each directory on PATH, left to right       4. the data directory (chd), where any readable file is run as          a procedure file     It prints the first answer and stops.  A file counts when it opens     in execute mode, so its attributes are judged exactly as the kernel     judges them when it loads a program.     A module in memory has no filename.  which prints its name, a tab,     and every file in the execution directory and on PATH that holds     the same module -- the same name, size and CRC -- whatever the file     is called:       $ load ckermit       $ which wermit       wermit  in memory, same as /dd/CMDS/ckermit     A module that matches no file there reads `in no file on the search     path'.  While it runs, which is in memory itself, so `which which'     always says so.OPTIONS     -a   print every place the name is found, in search order, instead          of stopping at the first     -i   read an alias or shell-function definition on standard input          and print it before anything else -- for shells that have          aliases, as in `alias | which -i name'     --   take the next argument as the command, even if it begins          with `-'     With no command, which prints the directories it searches after     the module directory -- the execution directory, then PATH -- one     per line.  A relative PATH entry is named from the execution     directory, which is where the shell resolves it.EXIT STATUS     0 if the command was found.  1 if not, and the directories searched     are listed on the error path.SEE ALSO     whereis, about, mdir, pdORIGIN     `fast which' version 6, posted to comp.sources.misc in February 1991     (volume 17, issue 12).  The OS-9 version adds the module directory,     the execution directory and the data directory, and decides what     may run by opening it.  Both sources are in SRC/which.